# Campus Köthen — assessed vulnerability exceptions for the published images.
#
# GitHub Actions builds each image locally, applies these narrowly documented
# exceptions, and fails on every other fixable HIGH/CRITICAL vulnerability
# before publishing to GHCR. Every entry carries a REVIEW-BY date so an
# exception cannot become permanent by accident.
#
# This file is NOT a place to quiet inconvenient findings. Every entry states
# what was checked, why the finding does not apply, and what keeps it from
# silently becoming real again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-14257 — brace-expansion: DoS via unbounded expansion length
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported against `brace-expansion 1.1.18` in the CMS image.
#
# Why it does not apply: the advisory publishes ONE major-spanning range
# ("<=5.0.7 vulnerable, >=5.0.8 fixed"), so it still matches the BACKPORTED
# releases on the 1.x and 2.x lines. The fix is demonstrably present in what we
# ship — 1.1.18 introduces EXPANSION_MAX_LENGTH plus the bounding check
# `if (length + expansion.length > maxLength) return out`, and its source
# comment names this very CVE.
#
# Why we do not simply upgrade to 5.x: v5 is an ESM-first rewrite whose
# CommonJS entry exports `{ expand }` instead of the function itself. The
# consumer here is `minimatch@3`, deep inside Strapi's own tree
# (@strapi/strapi -> fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin -> minimatch@3), and it
# calls the default export directly — verified by requiring the built package,
# which yields an object, not a callable. Forcing the major would turn a
# scanner warning into a real runtime failure.
#
# What keeps this honest: pnpm-workspace.yaml pins every affected major line to
# its patched minimum (>=1.1.17, >=2.1.3, >=5.0.8), so a genuinely vulnerable
# version cannot come back unnoticed. Re-check whenever the advisory range is
# corrected upstream or Strapi drops minimatch@3.
# REVIEW BY 2027-02-25 — or sooner if the advisory range is corrected upstream
# or Strapi drops minimatch@3.
CVE-2026-14257

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-40345 — deepmerge-ts: DoS via recursion limit in deepmerge
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported against `deepmerge-ts 7.1.5` in the backend image.
#
# Why it does not apply: deepmerge-ts is pulled in transitively by
# @prisma/config (via prisma and @prisma/client 7.9.0) solely to merge static
# Prisma configuration options at startup. It does not process untrusted
# external input, recursive client payload structures, or network requests.
#
# Why we do not simply upgrade to 8.x: v8 is a breaking major version. Prisma
# 7.9.0 explicitly depends on the 7.x line of @prisma/config and deepmerge-ts;
# forcing a major override risks breaking Prisma client initialization.
#
# What keeps this honest: the dependency is only invoked during Prisma config
# resolution at application startup. Re-check when Prisma updates its
# dependencies to a fixed release.
# REVIEW BY 2027-02-25 — or sooner once Prisma moves off the 7.x line of
# @prisma/config and deepmerge-ts.
CVE-2026-40345

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj / CVE-2026-59870 — js-yaml: quadratic CPU consumption
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported against `js-yaml 3.15.0` in the CMS image.
#
# Why it does not apply: js-yaml is pulled in transitively by Strapi's internal
# configuration tooling (via load-yaml-file). It is only invoked during boot to
# parse static repository configuration files, never to parse untrusted external
# user payloads or runtime network requests.
#
# Why we do not simply upgrade to 4.x: js-yaml v4 contains breaking changes and
# is required by upstream @strapi dependencies that specify ^3.13.0.
#
# What keeps this honest: no user-facing endpoint parses YAML at runtime.
# REVIEW BY 2027-02-25 — or sooner once upstream @strapi dependencies accept
# js-yaml 4.x.
GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj
CVE-2026-59870

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-67213 — nanoid: infinite loop in random ID generation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported against `nanoid 3.3.16` in the CMS image.
#
# Why it does not apply: nanoid is pulled in transitively by postcss inside
# Strapi's admin panel dependency tree solely for AST node ID generation during
# admin panel assets compilation. It is not used for security-sensitive token
# generation, cryptography, or user sessions.
#
# Why we do not simply upgrade: postcss pins nanoid ^3.3.x within its release.
#
# What keeps this honest: user session tokens and Strapi API tokens are generated
# using Node crypto and JWT secrets, not postcss's internal nanoid helper.
# REVIEW BY 2027-02-25 — or sooner once postcss unpins nanoid ^3.3.x.
CVE-2026-67213

